Purpose
Produce clear, working, testable, and maintainable solutions during interviews or coding tasks. Favor Python 3.12 with standard library only. Use Java 17 or TypeScript/Node 18 only if explicitly requested.

Core Principles
Default stack: Python 3.12, standard library only. Avoid external libraries unless the prompt explicitly allows and they are already installed.
Single-file solutions composed of small, pure functions with explicit type hints and docstrings.
Deterministic behavior: no network calls and no file I/O unless explicitly required. If randomness is necessary, seed it and make it explicit.
Treat the AI as a collaborator for boilerplate, alternatives, and test scaffolding, but you own design and correctness.
Interaction Model (Follow this for every task)
Clarify & Restate
Restate the problem in your own words.
Enumerate inputs and outputs precisely.
Note constraints (e.g., N, latency, memory).
Call out evaluation criteria and edge cases (empty inputs, singletons, duplicates, already-sorted, extremes, invalid values).
Propose a Plan
Example: “Baseline first, then add asserts for happy path and edge cases, finally refactor for clarity/performance.”
Code & Test Incrementally
Start with a minimal, correct happy-path implementation.
Immediately add 3–5 assert statements covering base cases and critical edge cases.
Keep functions small (<30 lines), pure, and single-responsibility.
Refactor & Optimize
Only after tests pass.
Extract helpers, improve naming, and explicitly state time/space complexity for the core logic.
Optimize only after establishing correctness.
Final Review
Run a quick self-check (see checklist).
Summarize trade-offs and note next steps you would take with more time.
Code Craftsmanship
Prefer this canonical flow when applicable:
parse_input(raw) -> structured_data
validate_input(data) -> None (raises on error)
solve_core(data) -> result
format_output(result) -> string
Use clear, consistent names; write comments to explain “why,” not “what.”
Avoid global mutable state; keep constants near where they are used.
Include type hints and concise docstrings on all public functions.
Performance & Data Structures
Analyze before optimizing; state time and space complexity of solve_core.
Avoid recomputation in loops; use iterators/generators for large data.
Choose data structures purposefully: sets/dicts for membership, heapq for top-K or streaming min/max, deque for BFS, union-find for connectivity.
Testing Guidance
Inline asserts immediately after writing the initial solution.
Cover: happy path, empty/null input, singleton, extremes, and a known tricky/edge case.
Keep tests deterministic and fast; no I/O or network in tests unless required by the prompt.
Response Pattern Templates
To Kick-off:
“Restate the problem with inputs/outputs/constraints/edge cases. Then outline a 5-step plan.”
For Implementation:
“Generate a pure Python solution using the standard library. Include type hints, docstrings, and 3–5 asserts covering the happy path and edge cases.”
For Refinement:
“Refactor this into small, composable functions with clear names. State the time/space complexity.”
For Optimization:
“Suggest a heap-based optimization for this code and show how to test that it doesn’t change correctness.”
Self-Review Checklist (quick pass before finishing)
Problem restated correctly with clear inputs/outputs and constraints.
Single-file, small pure functions with type hints and docstrings.
Deterministic: no unintended I/O or network; randomness seeded if used.
3–5 asserts cover happy path and edge cases.
Time and space complexity stated for the core algorithm.
Good names, minimal “why” comments, no unnecessary complexity.
Clear trade-offs and next steps summarized.
